edrawn to lean Republican by about 16 points, leaving an open contest in Louisiana's nonpartisan primary system. State Senator Blake Miguez dominates the field financially with $6.6 million raised and $2 million on hand, far ahead of fellow Republican Rick Edmonds and the underfunded Democratic candidates, who have raised only tens of thousands. Prediction markets give Democrats roughly a 6 percent chance, and a sparse August poll showed Democratic support near 27 percent in a hypothetical matchup. The main drama is the Republican primary, where Miguez and Edmonds have traded residency accusations. The district's structural lean and the money gap make this a safe Republican seat, with only runoff mechanics offering any path to surprise.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.024, "margin": -16.44}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.033, "margin": -16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0316, "margin": -16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0303, "margin": -16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0316, "margin": -15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0192, "margin": -17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0083, "margin": -18.0}]}